<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	kept's privacy manifest.

	Every entry here was derived from the code that actually ships, not from
	memory. `test/ios-privacy-manifest.test.ts` re-derives it on every run by
	walking the installed plugin sources for the symbols Apple's required-reason
	list names, so a plugin upgrade that starts touching a new category fails
	the suite instead of failing review.

	WHY THE APP DECLARES THE PLUGINS' USE. A pod that ships its own
	PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy answers for itself: @capacitor/ios does, and declares
	that it accesses nothing. @capacitor/camera, @capacitor/filesystem and
	@capacitor/local-notifications ship none at all, so whatever they touch is
	the app's to declare. That is the boundary the test walks.

	NSPrivacyTracking is false and both arrays are empty because they are true,
	not because they are the defaults: nothing leaves the device, there is no
	account, no analytics and no third party — which is the claim the Settings
	screen, the onboarding and the landing page all make, and which
	`e2e third-party` proves for the web build on every CI run.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!--
			File timestamps, reason C617.1: "files inside the app container".

			Measured, not assumed. @capacitor/filesystem calls
			`FileManager.default.attributesOfItem` and reads `.creationDate` and
			`.modificationDate` (Filesystem.swift:109, FilesystemPlugin.swift:214-260)
			to answer `stat` and `readdir`. kept reaches that plugin for the
			mirror, for receipt photographs and for an exported backup — all of
			them inside its own Documents directory and nowhere else, which is
			exactly what C617.1 covers. The other three reasons in that category
			are for files outside the container, which this app never touches.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
